I applaud the direction that OSEP is taking to focus on two different types of capacity - one technical, the other human, as a way to improve outcomes for all students, including those with disabilities. 

With respect to the question of whether OSEP create two centers, I recommend that you do offer two distinct Centers. Based on experiences working with States and school districts across the country within the area of special education data management, instruction, and interventions, there is tremendous value and need for two coordinated Centers in the areas described in the Federal Register.

With this in mind, I suggest that OSEP more clearly define the objectives these two Centers so that these respective technical and human capacity building investments are clear in their respective purpose and intent. Based on the Federal register and additional information found online, perhaps these focus areas could be defined more distinctly as building:

1) The technological capacity within and across states to collect, access and appropriately share high quality, timely data through improved tools and integration; and
2) The human capacity, awareness and skills of personnel at the SEA and LEA levels to more effectively analyze, access and apply data in efforts to improve policy, programs, placement and instructional practice

I would also recommend that OSEP consider the following with respect to both describing, awarding and evaluating both Centers: 
 Be more explicit about how this round of investments can help states and LEAs shift their mindset and build skills of administrators and practitioners (educators/providers) to focus more deliberately on student outcomes (i.e., progress monitoring) and other indicators of growth and student improvement;
 Reinforce the position that has been expressed by OSEP that students with disabilities can be held to high standards and succeed;
 Include requirements and guidance that would ensure strong communication and collaboration across the grantees/recipients of resulting grants/awards, so that investments in technology and human capacity are coordinated and aligned in vision and purpose;
 In determining the geographical focus of these investments, require that applicants for these awards describe how they will work with districts and local providers of technologies and professional development to leverage/focus existing capacity and expertise on a common vision;
 Prescribe within the NGO/RFP at least some specific outputs or objectives in each capacity building area that would help OSEP determine applicant success through these Centers;
 Consider including capacity building investments (technology, professional development, etc.) within each of the Centers that focus on parental access (technology), skills (training), understanding (communications), etc.;
 Require active participation in existing forums and groups (e.g., STATS-DC, CCSSO collaboratives, OSEP Leadership Conference, etc.) as a way to share information, progress, and solicit feedback; and
 Include in the NGO/RFP requirements that describe how these Centers will help identify, promote and harness best practices in service delivery and service documentation at the classroom and building level as a way to drive improvement efforts and engage local stakeholders in the process.

